# DISABLED EXAMPLE. Rename to 50-organization.caddy inside the gateway's
# /etc/caddy/routes.d/ to turn the organization hierarchy on; move it to off/
# and `caddy reload` to turn it off.
#
# Organization hierarchy (backend-rs/src/routes/org.rs, org_consolidated.rs):
#   links and grants    /api/v1/org/overview
#                       /api/v1/org/links              (GET, POST)
#                       /api/v1/org/links/accept       (POST)
#                       /api/v1/org/links/{id}         (DELETE)
#                       /api/v1/org/links/{id}/members (GET)
#                       /api/v1/org/links/{id}/members/{user_id} (PUT, DELETE)
#   consolidated reads  /api/v1/org/consolidated/{committed-demand,stock-signals,
#                                                purchase-orders,budgets}
#
# NO PYTHON FAILOVER. These routes exist only in Rust: there is no Python twin
# for the gateway to fall back to, and a Python answer would be a 404 that looks
# like "this feature is not there". So the group lists api-rs ONLY. With the Rust
# container down the gateway answers 502, which is the truth; the rest of the
# product is unaffected because the other route groups still fail over.
#
# The schema (org_links, org_link_grants) is created by the Python app's
# migrations at boot, and Python itself honours the feature on the paths it
# still serves: whole-tenant erasure, the data export and deactivating a person
# (backend/organizations/service.py). Turning this group off hides the screens'
# data; it does not delete links or grants.

@rs_organization {
	path /api/v1/org/*
}
handle @rs_organization {
	reverse_proxy api-rs:8021 {
		health_uri /health
		health_interval 5s
		health_timeout 2s
	}
}
